nted Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172049 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172049 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "337518a9-39a1-57e3-8686-74a135f7c3d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172178 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172178 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172178 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172178 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172178 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172178 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172178 #edb/ref 1045 "Simple, Closed, and Oriented Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172178 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172178 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172178 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "029bded9-b237-543e-8c70-78a922e3ce4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172664 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172664 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172664 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172664 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1b/1b0ced561cd98f9cf81c7de42bee7411a39595a9c59f2edaad0121d712dad157.png)\n\nWhich of the following curves is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172664 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8658a678-c6ba-5ca2-b63b-0885c74173b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172664 #edb/ref 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented.\n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve II is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172909 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172909 #edb/ref 1061 32008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172909 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7bafdfe3-0f81-5668-ad79-241cf2fc2b91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172909 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172916 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172916 #edb/ref 1061 32200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172916 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a0f9d7f-ab53-56ab-bdd3-8439d8f9ce7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172916 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172917 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172917 #edb/ref 1061 32201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172917 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b500b6d6-4ad6-5209-ba0d-8f0be8ba230d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172917 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172918 #edb/ref 1061 32202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172918 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01467366-cedf-5b66-ab24-41950e76376f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172918 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172929 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172929 #edb/ref 1061 32465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172929 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b76b9e7-6d5f-584c-837f-bd003bd3c835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172929 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173163 #edb/ref 1064 "A  plane curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r:[a,b]\\to \\Bbb R^2\n$$\n\nis said to be **simple** if it doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints. Notice that  this means that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t_1)\\neq \\mathbf r(t_2), \\qquad \na < t_1< t_2 < b.\n$$\n\nFor example, curve I shown below is simple while curve II is not simple.\n\n![](images/7b/7be1bc23d37c187a642d51917d6acb846147059bc6e9978d6ccd4ed35eed8794.png)\n\nA  plane curve is said to be **closed** if its terminal point coincides with its initial point, that is, if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(b)= \\mathbf r(a).\n$$\n\n![](images/4b/4b21a6e638cb8b0bae8967ba142fbfacb14349f34ff80d9359643e03ea1c1f83.png)\n\nFor example, curve III shown below is closed, while curve IV is not:\n\n![](images/85/8507fb039da42f37307ca3994cfb5ad66b288fb28d0cfd1a1a179acd2f83ae5e.png)\n\n**Watch out!** Curve III is an example of both a closed and simple curve. Notice that an example of a closed but *not* simple curve looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86dcd1dc864a2f0e2ceade072ba230b8fb4fe8923189a7eed2964f6f66a67651.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173163 #edb/ref 1065 5100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173163 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "72ccdc89-4807-5abb-9f69-7e017f195c3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173163 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173171 #edb/ref 1064 "A very important (although very hard to prove) result in mathematics states the following:\n\nAny simple and closed curve divides the plane into exactly two regions, one inside the curve and one outside.\n\nBased on this fact, we can define the **orientation** of a simple closed curve as follows:\n\nA simple closed curve is **positively oriented** if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is **negatively oriented**.\n\nFor example, curve I shown below is positively oriented, while curve II is negatively oriented:\n\n![](images/95/9527dd8bdfd979a1d521ccf945e84ef9b557d67e3d89ce8f5e4f7bb8d287ee8c.png)\n\nAn alternative convenient way of looking at simple oriented curves is that a curve is positively oriented if it is traversed *counterclockwise* and it is negatively oriented if it is traversed *clockwise*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173171 #edb/ref 1065 5165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173171 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4c2c2591-a914-53a3-9f60-6e7335d049f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173171 #edb/ref 1067 "Oriented Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361045 #edb/ref 1080 "images/cd/cd1f754fa00d1af2d8901ecc4c0cac434afb433474685fe7777d74f1703bafb2.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361045 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361045 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2eeddbc3-0c2b-51c9-a0c9-e864cef13cda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361085 #edb/ref 1080 "I and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361085 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361085 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "36b8832f-f459-53a5-bebf-7fce914129df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361243 #edb/ref 1080 "images/2d/2d352cf134fa7aa740d42f1de4e731cf9f53e26341a4ee3fefae809db0c8a3f1.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361243 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361243 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "59405ea1-d578-52de-82ff-d38f4fd2dabb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361274 #edb/ref 1080 "images/58/58efe73124a5f0fa8824ea05d118ea92ea1b30cf950f740c9c9b5376650044bd.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361274 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361274 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "617af790-b042-53c8-b62b-12dee18a22f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361325 #edb/ref 1080 "IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361325 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361325 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6d83564b-c398